{"Friedman's pieces are scattered and Newman has little difficulty in turning
this to account. A piquant sidelight, he actually effects a mate, the second
one in this tournament, and it was Friedman himself who, against Sergeant
administered the mate on the previous day:"} 26. Nb6 Rc4 27. Bxd6 {('27 B x
KtP' but this makes no sense at all)} Rf6 28. Nxc4 Nxc4 29. Bd5+ Be6 30. Bxc4
Bxc4 31. Bxe5 Re6 32. Qg4 b5 ({The score gives 'P–Kt4' and I spent some
fruitless time trying to figure out what on earth was happening, with the
further puzzling move/comment/typo '33. Q x P x no mate', until it dawned on
me that Black had probably blundered with 32...b5 and left mate in one en
prise! A reminder of just how annoying descriptive notation is... - JS} 32...
g5 {isn't much better as White plays} 33. Qxc4 {after which Black would surely
resign.}) 33. Qxg7# {Source: CHESS, Vol.15, no.169, October 1949, p27-28} 1-0

